{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_566","type":"reasoning","text":"The `.bin` directory and `.pnpm` symlinks got partially damaged. Let me check what's actually missing. The shared repo is not the trial workspace — it's the codebase owner's actual worktree used to host `node_modules`. I damaged it by my junction. I need to assess and, if possible, repair.\n\nActually wait — importantly, the task workspace (the trial `agent` dir) has NO node_modules and I never touched it. The damage is to `C:\\Users\\27933\\.codex\\worktrees\\tf0604-rsi-baseline\\ClawX\\node_modules` — a scratch dependency root used by the RSI harness (`nodeModulesPath` in manifest points there). Let me check the actual scope of damage: `.bin` is gone, `tsgo` gone, some dirs may be gone.\n\nLet me compare against what should be there. Actually — is `.bin` critical? pnpm puts `.bin` as a directory of symlinks. But wait, in pnpm the real packages live under `.pnpm/` and top-level `node_modules/<pkg>` are symlinks into `.pnpm`. So the top-level entries being \"directories\" here... let me check whether they're symlinks or real dirs.\n\nHmm, actually the listing shows Mode didn't render. Let me check more carefully whether the top-level entries are symlinks (intact) or whether real content was deleted.\n\nKey question: did `git worktree remove` delete directory contents through the junction? `.bin` is gone which suggests yes — it deleted the contents of `node_modules` recursively (which via junction = the shared node_modules), but `git` only deletes files it thinks are tracked/untracked in the worktree... Actually `git worktree remove` removes the whole worktree directory. Since `rsi-l01-gatecheck/node_modules` is a junction, and git's cleanup walks the directory tree...\n\nActually `git worktree remove` on Windows with a junction: git would try to delete the worktree dir. The junction is not a tracked file. Git's recursive delete might follow it (since it's a directory-like reparse point). That's the accident.\n\nLet me determine precisely what's missing now vs. before. Let me check .pnpm integrity and whether vitest still resolves.\n\nLet me count .pnpm entries and test if a simple node require works.\n\nActually, the most reliable repair: this is a pnpm store-based install. The `.pnpm` dir holds the actual packages. If `.pnpm` is intact, then `node_modules/<pkg>` symlinks can be recreated by `pnpm install --offline`. But that requires network/cache.\n\nLet me first assess precisely."}}
